1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,120
These high-end holdings are the province of the super-rich.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,600
Priceless possessions that allow wealthy men to indulge their passions,

3
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:13,700
harness their obsessions, and flex their mechanical muscles.

4
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:20,260
Exclusivity, technological complexity, manly competition, and just plain fun

5
00:00:20,261 --> 00:00:26,540
have driven many kingly consumers to covet, create, and purchase these outrageous items.

6
00:00:26,541 --> 00:00:33,540
They come with million dollar tech specs that aren't necessarily measured by cost, but by a state of mind.

7
00:00:33,541 --> 00:00:38,540
Male aspirations to own the biggest, the baddest, the best.

8
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:46,540
I think over the centuries, men of wealth have surrounded themselves with all types of toys

9
00:00:46,541 --> 00:00:49,540
just to differentiate themselves from the masses.

10
00:00:49,541 --> 00:00:54,540
And basically, you know, it just boils down to I do it because I can.

11
00:00:54,541 --> 00:00:56,420
and they want to show that power.

12
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,280
But there's a lot of hard work and there's a lot of thought

13
00:00:59,281 --> 00:01:00,540
and there's a lot of ingenuity.

14
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,920
There's a lot of art and craftsmanship that go into making these things.

15
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,460
Many of these power playthings have involved transportation,

16
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:15,400
allowing moguls to travel fast, in luxury, and with increased privacy.

17
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:20,460
At the turn of the century, many wealthy American industrialists,

18
00:01:20,461 --> 00:01:25,100
such as John D. Rockefeller, Andrew Carnegie, Henry Ford, and Jay Gould

19
00:01:25,101 --> 00:01:28,360
were renowned for their opulent personal railway cars,

20
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,440
a forerunner of today's private corporate jet.

21
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:36,440
Gould's rail car, the St. Louis, had ornate lighting fixtures,

22
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:40,240
vaulted ceilings, and an elaborate carved wood decor.

23
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,340
Today, there are about 80 private railway cars operating in the U.S.

24
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:54,580
John Kirkwood owns Eight, which he rents to well-heeled customers.

25
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:59,580
For personal travel, he hooks up a car to the back of Amtrak and takes off.

26
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:06,860
When you're on a train, there's a romance and a feel and just a texture of it that's just delightful.

27
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:12,420
From the smells to the sounds to the way it rides, it just gets in your blood.

28
00:02:12,421 --> 00:02:23,680
Kirkwood recently purchased and renovated the Bella Vista, a 1955 Union Pacific rail car at a cost of more than $1 million.

29
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:30,500
We took the car basically as a shell and replaced everything or rebuilt it or redesigned it.

30
00:02:30,501 --> 00:02:33,500
We put in a new kitchen from the ground up.

31
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,780
We had to design it. We had to select all the appliances.

32
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:38,720
We had to build the stainless steel counters and put the whole thing in.

33
00:02:38,721 --> 00:02:44,680
And then going back from there, we have all of the air conditioning and electrical cabinet, which is very complex.

34
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:48,880
There are about 35 miles of wire in this car.

35
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:58,760
The deluxe rail car has four wood-paneled bedrooms, some with tiled bathrooms, brass fixtures, and stylish ceiling fans.

36
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:04,700
The carpets are a thick Scottish blend, the pinched blinds custom-made,

37
00:03:04,701 --> 00:03:09,100
and the rich table linen ordered from an exclusive manufacturer in England.

38
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:14,200
A coveted feature is a dining area on the upper level for 20 people

39
00:03:14,201 --> 00:03:19,660
that sits under a glass dome affording a spectacular 360-degree view.

40
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:29,760
We'll be leaving Los Angeles this evening, going out to San Bernardino,

41
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:31,900
and then we'll climb up over to Home Pass.

42
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:33,160
That'll be after dark.

43
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:40,080
and they would not allow conversation is probably the biggest thing that people do on the train

44
00:03:40,081 --> 00:03:42,380
because they don't see each other the same way elsewhere.

45
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,760
Railway cars may be relaxing and nostalgic,

46
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:50,900
but if you're a man on the move with no time to spare,

47
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:52,840
how about a private helicopter?

48
00:03:55,180 --> 00:04:00,100
In 1923, Spain's Juan de la Sierra invented the autogyro,

49
00:04:00,101 --> 00:04:03,640
which could fly short distances using an overhead rotor.

50
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,900
But it could not float or land vertically.

51
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:17,920
Then, in 1939, Russian immigrant Igor Sikorsky created a rotor with a turbine engine that allowed a helicopter to hover.

52
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:24,140
For decades, helicopters were reserved for military operations.

53
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:30,900
But in the late 1970s, the Sikorsky S-76 became one of the first choppers used by civilians.

54
00:04:30,901 --> 00:04:38,760
A favorite of the busy Fortune 500 executive, it could soar at 180 miles per hour and cost

55
00:04:38,761 --> 00:04:42,080
about $1 million.

56
00:04:42,081 --> 00:04:47,360
Today there are about 2,000 helicopters in America licensed for private use, and about

57
00:04:47,361 --> 00:04:51,040
5% are used for pleasure.

58
00:04:51,041 --> 00:04:58,160
One big bird of choice is the Eurocopter A-Star 350B2, a 47-foot long chopper that can carry

59
00:04:58,161 --> 00:04:59,880
up to six people, including the pilot.

60
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:05,260
It's all about time and efficiency, and the helicopter provides you that, and especially

61
00:05:05,261 --> 00:05:06,220
off-site landings.

62
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:09,600
Flying a helicopter is much more fun, much more challenging than the plane.

63
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:14,740
Sometimes you'll land on a mountaintop or a building top or on location or at a polo

64
00:05:14,741 --> 00:05:19,100
field or at a private house, and it's just the fact that every time you land and you

65
00:05:19,101 --> 00:05:20,520
go someplace new, it is new.

66
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:24,220
Where at airplanes, you land at one airport, it's like landing at any other airport.

67
00:05:24,221 --> 00:05:33,160
Peter McCarnan leases helicopters and in his spare time loves to use his $1.8 million fly

68
00:05:33,161 --> 00:05:35,160
toy.

69
00:05:35,161 --> 00:05:41,820
It features a dual digital autopilot system, integrated avionics, and an all-glass cockpit

70
00:05:41,821 --> 00:05:45,960
for flying in varied weather.

71
00:05:45,961 --> 00:05:52,280
The helicopter also houses a small but powerful 800-horsepower jet turbine engine and comes

72
00:05:52,281 --> 00:05:55,400
with lightweight aluminum and graphite rotors and blades.

73
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:59,120
All that translates into speed.

74
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:02,500
I probably can get 155 miles an hour on this aircraft,

75
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,680
and an aircraft of 10 years ago, maybe 120.

76
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,280
I have the greatest of navigational equipment.

77
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,500
I have global positioning, satellite positioning on the dashboard.

78
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:11,980
I have dual comms.

79
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:15,300
And then on the other side, I have air conditioning and leather seats and berber carpet.

80
00:06:15,301 --> 00:06:17,760
So it's a good mesh on both sides of my world.

81
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:22,060
For those who prefer to play on terra firma,

82
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:26,280
Motorcycles have roared up roads for more than a century.

83
00:06:26,281 --> 00:06:31,280
One of the first was built by German inventor Gottlieb Daimler in 1885.

84
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:40,280
But its engine, which had a surface carburetor with an open pool of gasoline heated by an adjacent flame, proved too dangerous.

85
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:49,280
In 1895, a much safer internal combustion single cylinder engine was perfected by a French company, Didion Bouton.

86
00:06:49,281 --> 00:06:56,500
Fuel was ignited in an enclosed cylinder, and as it expanded, pushed on a piston.

87
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,720
A design that hasn't changed much since that time.

88
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,800
Today, motorcycles come in all shapes and sizes.

89
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:05,860
Just ask Arlen Ness.

90
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:14,420
In the mid-1960s, the motorbike buff first constructed his own hog around a 1947 Harley-Davidson knucklehead engine,

91
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:16,880
and layered it with outrageous accessories.

92
00:07:16,881 --> 00:07:27,300
I had customized it with putting a small tank on it and tall handlebars, a metal flake paint job, upsweep pipes.

93
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:35,260
The bike, Ness's Untouchable, started the whole custom-made motorcycle craze in the U.S.

94
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:41,940
Arlen Ness Enterprises now designs and manufactures more than 2,000 different motorcycle accessories

95
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:45,460
and hand-builds about 50 custom-made hogs per year.

96
00:07:45,461 --> 00:07:46,600
Much better.

97
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:53,880
Each can take up to six months to construct and cost up to $100,000.

98
00:07:53,881 --> 00:07:57,880
When you're building a custom bike, it's like from scratch.

99
00:07:57,881 --> 00:08:03,880
You start with bare engine cases and the motor completely disassembled and a transmission.

100
00:08:03,881 --> 00:08:10,880
You're assembling and building and polishing and rubbing and welding on every little piece that goes on it.

101
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:16,720
When I first started doing this, I just did it because I love tinkering and working with my hands.

102
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,100
Yeah, so in the center of the spring, so I have to get winded.

103
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:24,680
I started making a few custom parts, and they were getting accepted real well,

104
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:31,640
and so we started making some more, and gosh, it turned into a real business beyond my wildest dreams.

105
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:38,820
The Nest bikes are mobile art, flashy showcases for those who love riding on road heaven.

106
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:45,520
Ness has his own mini-museum above his shop, which houses some older bikes,

107
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:51,260
and on street level displays such custom classics as the Corvaceous,

108
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:56,320
the Flame Chopper,

109
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:58,640
the Stretch,

110
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:01,800
the Fire Engine,

111
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:06,180
and the Ferrari.

112
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:09,440
I built that about the same time the Testarossa's come out,

113
00:09:09,441 --> 00:09:13,620
with side scoops and of course painted a Ferrari red.

114
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:18,240
Built rear wheel was about the size of the tire on the back of a Ferrari.

115
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:24,780
That particular bike has a real powerful motor with two superchargers, four Weber carburetors.

116
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:31,600
Ness spends most of his time running his multi-million dollar business in San Leandro, California.

117
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:37,420
Designing accessories, building and testing his motorcycles and leading bike runs.

118
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:43,520
But Ness's motorbikes seem routine when compared to the lavish luxuries of the past

119
00:09:43,521 --> 00:09:51,340
Next, ancient barges, complex clocks, Fabergé's special eggs

120
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:54,880
No object was too costly for history's kings

121
00:09:54,881 --> 00:10:02,920
As of 2001, the Sikorsky S-76 private chopper costs about $8.5 million

122
00:10:02,921 --> 00:10:05,160
And can carry six passengers

123
00:10:05,161 --> 00:10:08,240
Donald Trump and the Queen of England are proud owners.

124
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:11,780
Million Dollar Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

125
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:16,100
Before today's humble extravagances,

126
00:10:16,740 --> 00:10:19,620
there were the pleasure-packed possessions of ancient royalty.

127
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:23,260
Many kings and emperors spared no expense

128
00:10:23,261 --> 00:10:26,180
in collecting extravagant, ego-boosting objects

129
00:10:26,181 --> 00:10:30,540
to highlight the technological and military power of their fiefdoms

130
00:10:30,541 --> 00:10:33,040
and the personal omnipotence of their rule.

131
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:40,000
In 37 AD, the Roman Emperor Caligula built large royal barges,

132
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:45,980
complete with intricate tiled mosaics, massive flags and banners, and flowing fountains.

133
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:51,620
The recreational boats were about 234 feet long by 70 feet wide.

134
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:56,340
They had a radiant heating system that employed slaves using bellows

135
00:10:56,341 --> 00:11:00,180
to pump hot air from a furnace through terracotta tubes in the walls.

136
00:11:00,181 --> 00:11:05,340
Special dining areas were constructed that could accommodate hundreds of people.

137
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:17,400
The corrupt Caligula, who once made his favorite horse a senator, was eventually murdered by his Praetorian guard, and his barges sunk by his successor, Claudius.

138
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:22,560
Other rulers preferred to use their treasured objects on the ground.

139
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:30,120
In 1953, Britain's Queen Elizabeth II traveled to her coronation in the gold state coach.

140
00:11:30,180 --> 00:11:36,000
originally constructed for her ancestor King George the third in 1762 who built

141
00:11:36,001 --> 00:11:42,600
it to outdo his royal rivals the four-ton gilded carriage is 12 feet high

142
00:11:42,601 --> 00:11:47,700
24 feet long and is topped by three cherubs holding the crown and assorted

143
00:11:47,701 --> 00:11:55,800
regalia the richly carved coach body is supported by braces covered in Moroccan

144
00:11:55,801 --> 00:11:59,148
leather and ornamented with gilt buckles.

145
00:11:59,149 --> 00:12:02,740
The carriage has been used for every British

146
00:12:02,741 --> 00:12:04,921
coronation since 1821.

147
00:12:04,922 --> 00:12:11,860
By the late 19th century, kings across Europe were still vying for the

148
00:12:11,861 --> 00:12:17,600
most spectacular collections of jewelry, antiques, and precious items to show off their pomp and

149
00:12:17,601 --> 00:12:18,318
circumstance.

150
00:12:18,319 --> 00:12:22,680
Traditionally in Russia during Easter, people exchange Easter eggs, and they're

151
00:12:22,681 --> 00:12:25,097
symbol of Christ and rebirth.

152
00:12:25,098 --> 00:12:29,680
In 1885, Carl Fabergé, the most famous court jeweler in

153
00:12:29,820 --> 00:12:35,360
history, began making a special series of annual Easter eggs for his patron, Tsar Alexander

154
00:12:35,361 --> 00:12:39,404
III of Russia. Inside each egg was a small surprise.

155
00:12:39,405 --> 00:12:42,360
Each year, the mechanisms or surprises

156
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:44,390
became more complicated.

157
00:12:44,391 --> 00:12:47,720
That was what people looked forward to, was what the surprise was

158
00:12:47,721 --> 00:12:48,843
inside of the egg.

159
00:12:48,844 --> 00:12:52,960
Some of the more unique surprises were what are called automatons.

160
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:57,200
They're small mechanical objects that when wound up would move, walk, etc.

161
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:04,820
When Nicholas II inherited the throne after his father's death in 1894, he expanded the

162
00:13:04,821 --> 00:13:07,953
tradition by ordering two eggs for each Easter.

163
00:13:07,954 --> 00:13:10,820
Every egg was impossibly intricate and took

164
00:13:10,821 --> 00:13:13,936
more than 18 months to create and build.

165
00:13:13,937 --> 00:13:17,520
In all, 50 imperial eggs were completed before

166
00:13:17,521 --> 00:13:19,474
the fall of the Tsar in 1917.

167
00:13:19,475 --> 00:13:23,720
Fabergé had the unique ability to bring together craftsmen from

168
00:13:23,721 --> 00:13:28,820
different disciplines and to have them work cohesively together to create these eggs.

169
00:13:28,821 --> 00:13:29,521
So

170
00:13:29,522 --> 00:13:35,380
they involved engravers, stone setters, stone cutters, enamelists, goldsmiths, silversmiths,

171
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:40,072
etc. All working as one unit to create these works of art.

172
00:13:40,073 --> 00:13:42,580
One of the most spectacular was

173
00:13:42,581 --> 00:13:48,100
the winter egg, which consisted of two blocks of carved rock crystal, overlaid with platinum

174
00:13:48,101 --> 00:13:52,434
and diamonds, to give the appearance of frost.

175
00:13:52,435 --> 00:13:56,580
In 1994, it sold for $5.5 million at auction

176
00:13:56,581 --> 00:13:57,815
in the West.

177
00:13:57,816 --> 00:14:05,640
While the Tsar was losing his eggs, a New York banking tycoon, Henry Graves,

178
00:14:05,641 --> 00:14:07,840
and carmaker James Ward Packard

179
00:14:07,841 --> 00:14:11,140
were busy proving that time is money.

180
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:14,720
The two business titans got into a friendly war

181
00:14:14,721 --> 00:14:18,200
over who could purchase the most elaborate and expensive pocket watch.

182
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:22,100
Their yardstick was the number of complications.

183
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:25,480
By complications, we mean functions

184
00:14:25,481 --> 00:14:29,140
other than just telling the hours and the minutes on a watch.

185
00:14:29,141 --> 00:14:33,780
A typical complication would be a day symbol, a date symbol,

186
00:14:33,781 --> 00:14:35,560
a function of start and stop.

187
00:14:37,220 --> 00:14:40,040
For several years, Packard led the competition

188
00:14:40,041 --> 00:14:42,960
by owning a 16-complication pocket watch.

189
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:48,920
But in 1928, Graves went to Swiss watchmaker Patek Philippe

190
00:14:48,921 --> 00:14:52,980
and commissioned an unheard-of 24-complication watch

191
00:14:52,981 --> 00:14:55,580
as a way to best his horological rival.

192
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:00,040
The watch design included interweaving intricate layers

193
00:15:00,041 --> 00:15:03,940
of more than 900 mechanical parts into a tiny space,

194
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:06,940
synchronized to display exact time elements.

195
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:11,840
The watch has two faces, one on the front and one on the back.

196
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:15,860
On the front of the watch, you have your basic time functions.

197
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:19,040
It has a dual stopwatch. It has the day and the date.

198
00:15:19,300 --> 00:15:22,560
It also has a minute repeater with a Westminster chime, which is quite unique.

199
00:15:23,080 --> 00:15:25,420
On the opposite side is sidereal time.

200
00:15:25,421 --> 00:15:31,640
The central portion of the watch is an enamel plaque that illustrates the night sky over New York City.

201
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,200
It's a rotational map that charts the stars throughout the whole year.

202
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:42,420
The one-pound pocket watch took more than five years to design and build.

203
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:46,260
After several years, Graves passed the watch on to his heirs.

204
00:15:46,261 --> 00:15:55,700
In December 1999, the Graves Super Complication Pocket Watch sold for $11 million at auction.

205
00:15:56,500 --> 00:16:04,140
That princely sum can buy 560 of these all-gold Rolex watches, which are only $20,000 apiece.

206
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:11,080
Other power possessions that have soared in value are guns.

207
00:16:11,081 --> 00:16:17,080
San Francisco resident Greg Martin, a collector who is also a consultant to the auction house Butterfields,

208
00:16:17,081 --> 00:16:22,080
owns one of the most valuable private firearm collections in the U.S.

209
00:16:22,081 --> 00:16:31,080
Arms in general, particularly guns, embrace all the elements of the decorative arts.

210
00:16:31,081 --> 00:16:38,080
You have engraving, you have jewel work, you have inlays of precious metals.

211
00:16:38,081 --> 00:16:43,740
So you have all the elements, not only of the decorative arts, but of mechanical genius combined.

212
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:53,540
One of Greg Martin's favorite revolvers is a $1.5 million gold inlaid Colt 1849 pocket model,

213
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:57,000
etched by master engraver Gustav Jung.

214
00:16:58,460 --> 00:17:01,320
Only about two dozen are known to be in existence.

215
00:17:01,321 --> 00:17:10,680
Gustav Jung used every area of space and intricately engraved this with beautiful floral design.

216
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:18,700
No one would ever fire this gun because, like coins, in best condition, commands the best price.

217
00:17:18,701 --> 00:17:24,020
And guns, surprisingly enough, that are not fired are the most coveted by collectors.

218
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:28,900
Another of Martin's favorite guns is the Colt Dragoon.

219
00:17:28,901 --> 00:17:33,380
At four and a half pounds, the largest handgun ever manufactured.

220
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:41,380
Martin also collects other weapons, and one treasured marvel is the largest sword ever made in America.

221
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:50,900
The fearsome blade was made by the Ames Manufacturing Company in Massachusetts in the 1830s for a very tall Kentuckian.

222
00:17:51,380 --> 00:17:53,409
My favorite sword belonged to a giant.

223
00:17:53,410 --> 00:17:57,360
He was named James Porter, the Kentucky Giant, and he was seven feet nine.

224
00:17:57,361 --> 00:18:02,980
and Charles Dickens wrote about him in his American travels in 1840s

225
00:18:02,981 --> 00:18:08,360
