442
00:38:03,481 --> 00:38:10,240
One favorite is The Rock, a computer-controlled home mountain climbing system with interlocking

443
00:38:10,241 --> 00:38:17,000
panels that can simulate 30 different rock climbing routes.

444
00:38:17,001 --> 00:38:21,720
The structure stands about 10 feet high, is made of steel and aluminum, and is covered

445
00:38:21,721 --> 00:38:26,720
with sand and resin knobs that replicate rock footholds.

446
00:38:26,721 --> 00:38:28,720
Users can also create their own routes.

447
00:38:28,721 --> 00:38:31,920
On my PC in my office, I can make up a route.

448
00:38:32,680 --> 00:38:35,200
And we just design it and put it into the rock and climb it.

449
00:38:35,620 --> 00:38:37,160
It's like a Stairmaster on steroids.

450
00:38:37,860 --> 00:38:40,880
You do your hands, your feet, and your mind.

451
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:46,180
A digitally controlled dual motor system drives the speed of the panels

452
00:38:46,181 --> 00:38:48,540
that are pushed vertically by fan belts.

453
00:38:50,660 --> 00:38:55,300
Adventurers can climb thousands of feet in their living rooms while three feet off the ground,

454
00:38:55,820 --> 00:38:57,640
all for about $14,500.

455
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:03,720
But boy toys are not all about physical recreation.

456
00:39:03,721 --> 00:39:13,720
For those seeking a higher, more cerebral plane, look no further than New York orthodontist Ron Cole's 32-foot-high backyard observatory.

457
00:39:13,721 --> 00:39:23,720
Inside is one of the world's most powerful private telescopes, the CCT-20, an instrument with a 20-inch aperture.

458
00:39:23,721 --> 00:39:27,720
Price? $225,000.

459
00:39:27,721 --> 00:39:33,320
Well, a dome protects you from the elements. It protects your telescope.

460
00:39:33,321 --> 00:39:34,720
You can't collapse

461
00:39:34,980 --> 00:39:39,990
the telescope and take it inside every night. It's too large, too heavy.

462
00:39:39,991 --> 00:39:41,660
You want the environment

463
00:39:41,661 --> 00:39:45,234
in the dome exactly the same as the outside environment.

464
00:39:45,235 --> 00:39:47,660
You want the same ambient temperature.

465
00:39:47,661 --> 00:39:50,840
Otherwise, when you open the dome, if it's a difference in temperature, you have air

466
00:39:50,841 --> 00:39:54,900
movement which makes your image jump around.

467
00:39:54,901 --> 00:40:00,020
The telescope is 4,400 times more powerful at gathering light than the human eye.

468
00:40:00,680 --> 00:40:07,580
It is more than 10 feet high, has a 40 by 60 inch base, which is fortified in 35 tons of concrete.

469
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:12,900
Kohl's telescope is completely controlled by a computer in a separate room.

470
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:18,300
As far as finding objects in the sky, we used to have to go out with a telescope

471
00:40:18,301 --> 00:40:23,600
and manually move the telescope around and hop around the sky finding different objects.

472
00:40:23,601 --> 00:40:26,380
and you had to know where to look for the objects.

473
00:40:26,920 --> 00:40:30,140
Now, all you have to know is what you want to see.

474
00:40:30,460 --> 00:40:33,260
We go on a virtual sky on a computer screen.

475
00:40:33,880 --> 00:40:37,200
We move the mouse and click on any object we want to see.

476
00:40:37,500 --> 00:40:39,720
Telescope immediately moves to that object.

477
00:40:39,920 --> 00:40:44,340
Once you're on that object, it will track and follow that object all night long.

478
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:48,320
It has servo motors that counteract the rotation of the Earth

479
00:40:48,321 --> 00:40:51,780
so that your object stays in the field of view all night long.

480
00:40:51,781 --> 00:40:52,540
Just stop it.

481
00:40:53,600 --> 00:41:00,180
The telescope comes with a concave primary mirror, which reflects and focuses light onto a secondary mirror,

482
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:02,880
that then bounces light towards a peephole.

483
00:41:04,440 --> 00:41:09,420
But today's astronomers rarely look through the eyepiece on a telescope when viewing a distant galaxy.

484
00:41:10,040 --> 00:41:16,600
You can see it with your eye looking through the eyepiece, but it's a small, fuzzy little wisp of cotton.

485
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:17,960
You can't see detail.

486
00:41:17,961 --> 00:41:25,780
If you take the eyepiece off and put the CCD camera on and open the shutter for probably

487
00:41:25,781 --> 00:41:32,380
five, six minutes, then all that information is dumped back into your computer in the control

488
00:41:32,381 --> 00:41:33,081
room.

489
00:41:33,240 --> 00:41:38,480
You can see so much more because your eye can only see the photons of light that are falling

490
00:41:38,481 --> 00:41:39,840
on the retina at that instant.

491
00:41:40,940 --> 00:41:47,180
And up on the screen comes this gorgeous spiral galaxy with the arms and the dust lanes.

492
00:41:47,960 --> 00:41:54,960
The chip in the digital camera's vacuum chamber is kept at about 70 below zero Fahrenheit

493
00:41:54,961 --> 00:41:56,960
to increase sensitivity.

494
00:41:56,961 --> 00:42:00,960
The cost of the camera alone is about $17,000.

495
00:42:00,961 --> 00:42:07,960
You can do in five minutes now what it used to take the large telescopes on Earth hours to do

496
00:42:07,961 --> 00:42:12,960
because of the sensitivity of the CCD chip versus silver halide film.

497
00:42:12,961 --> 00:42:18,960
Besides recording thousands of pictures of spiral galaxies, globular clusters and nebulae,

498
00:42:18,961 --> 00:42:23,960
Dr. Cole has also received credit for independently discovering a new supernova.

499
00:42:23,961 --> 00:42:31,960
When I look out at these galaxies and I see they're very majestic and they're incredible.

500
00:42:31,961 --> 00:42:35,960
But yet what amazes me much more than that is the human mind.

501
00:42:35,961 --> 00:42:39,960
A galaxy is just the raw materials.

502
00:42:39,961 --> 00:42:44,180
we have evolved so that we are aware of all of this.

503
00:42:45,120 --> 00:42:46,320
I think that's pretty magnificent.

504
00:42:47,980 --> 00:42:53,280
For hundreds of years, these million-dollar tech toys have provided recreational pleasure,

505
00:42:53,880 --> 00:42:56,440
expanded the mind, and provoked our consciousness.

506
00:42:57,360 --> 00:43:04,040
They have stimulated, entertained, and been ridiculed by a public both admiring and envious.

507
00:43:04,180 --> 00:43:09,500
To their adult owners, these power playthings are a fulfillment of childhood dreams,

508
00:43:09,960 --> 00:43:15,080
Fantasies that have pushed the real boundaries of mechanics, science and technology

509
00:43:15,081 --> 00:43:19,400
and transformed lots of hard work into endless play.

